.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_CTX_SET_TMP_DH_CALLBACK 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_CTX_set_dh_auto ,
.Nm SSL_set_dh_auto ,
.Nm SSL_CTX_set0_tmp_dh_pkey ,
.Nm SSL_set0_tmp_dh_pkey ,
.Nm SSL_CTX_set_tmp_dh_callback ,
.Nm SSL_CTX_set_tmp_dh ,
.Nm SSL_set_tmp_dh_callback ,
.Nm SSL_set_tmp_dh
.Nd handle DH keys for ephemeral key exchange
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_CTX_set_dh_auto
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "int onoff"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_set_dh_auto
.Fa "SSL *s"
.Fa "int onoff"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_CTX_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "EVP_PKEY *dhpkey"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
.Fa "SSL *s"
.Fa "EVP_PKEY *dhpkey"
.Fc
The following functions have been deprecated since OpenSSL 3.0, and can
be hidden entirely by defining
.Ar OPENSSL_API_COMPAT
with a suitable version value, see
.Xr openssl_user_macros 7 :
.Ft void
.Fo SSL_CTX_set_tmp_dh_callback
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "DH *(*tmp_dh_callback)(SSL *ssl"
.Fa "int is_export"
.Fa "int keylength)"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_CTX_set_tmp_dh
.Fa "SSL_CTX *ctx"
.Fa "DH *dh"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo SSL_set_tmp_dh_callback
.Fa "SSL *ctx"
.Fa "DH *(*tmp_dh_callback)(SSL *ssl"
.Fa "int is_export"
.Fa "int keylength)"
.Fc
.Ft long
.Fo SSL_set_tmp_dh
.Fa "SSL *ssl"
.Fa "DH *dh"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The functions described on this page are relevant for servers only.
.Pp
Some ciphersuites may use ephemeral Diffie-Hellman (DH) key exchange.
In these cases, the session data is negotiated using the
ephemeral/temporary DH key and the key supplied and certified by the
certificate chain is only used for signing.
Anonymous ciphers (without a permanent server key) also use ephemeral DH
keys.
.Pp
Using ephemeral DH key exchange yields forward secrecy as the connection
can only be decrypted when the DH key is known.
By generating a temporary DH key inside the server application that is
lost when the application is left, it becomes impossible for an attacker
to decrypt past sessions, even if they get hold of the normal
(certified) key, as this key was only used for signing.
.Pp
In order to perform a DH key exchange the server must use a DH group (DH
parameters) and generate a DH key.
The server will always generate a new DH key during the negotiation.
.Pp
As generating DH parameters is extremely time consuming, an application
should not generate the parameters on the fly.
DH parameters can be reused, as the actual key is newly generated during
the negotiation.
.Pp
Typically applications should use well known DH parameters that have
built-in support in OpenSSL. The macros
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_dh_auto
and
.Fn SSL_set_dh_auto
configure OpenSSL to use the default built-in DH parameters for the
.Vt SSL_CTX
and
.Vt SSL
objects respectively.
Passing a value of 2 or 1 in the
.Fa onoff
parameter switches it on.
If the
.Fa onoff
parameter is set to 2, it will force the DH key size to 1024 if the
.Vt SSL_CTX
or
.Vt SSL
security level
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_security_level 3
is 0 or 1. Passing a value of 0 switches it off.
The default setting is off.
.Pp
If "auto" DH parameters are switched on then the parameters will be
selected to be consistent with the size of the key associated with the
server's certificate.
If there is no certificate (e.g.
for PSK ciphersuites), then it it will be consistent with the size of
the negotiated symmetric cipher key.
.Pp
Applications may supply their own DH parameters instead of using the
built-in values.
This approach is discouraged and applications should in preference use
the built-in parameter support described above.
Applications wishing to supply their own DH parameters should call
.Fn SSL_CTX_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
or
.Fn SSL_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
to supply the parameters for the
.Vt SSL_CTX
or
.Vt SSL
respectively.
The parameters should be supplied in the
.Fa dhpkey
argument as an
.Vt EVP_PKEY
containing DH parameters.
Ownership of the
.Fa dhpkey
value is passed to the
.Vt SSL_CTX
or
.Vt SSL
object as a result of this call, and so the caller should not free it if
the function call is successful.
.Pp
The deprecated macros
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_tmp_dh
and
.Fn SSL_set_tmp_dh
do the same thing as
.Fn SSL_CTX_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
and
.Fn SSL_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
except that the DH parameters are supplied in a
.Vt DH
object instead in the
.Fa dh
argument, and ownership of the
.Vt DH
object is retained by the application.
Applications should use "auto" parameters instead, or call
.Fn SSL_CTX_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
or
.Fn SSL_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
as appropriate.
.Pp
An application may instead specify the DH parameters via a callback
function using the functions
.Fn SSL_CTX_set_tmp_dh_callback
or
.Fn SSL_set_tmp_dh_callback
to set the callback for the
.Vt SSL_CTX
or
.Vt SSL
object respectively.
These functions are deprecated.
Applications should instead use "auto" parameters, or specify the
parameters via
.Fn SSL_CTX_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
or
.Fn SSL_set0_tmp_dh_pkey
as appropriate.
.Pp
The callback will be invoked during a connection when DH parameters are
required.
The
.Vt SSL
object for the current connection is supplied as an argument.
Previous versions of OpenSSL used the
.Fa is_export
and
.Sy keylength
arguments to control parameter generation for export and non-export
cipher suites.
Modern OpenSSL does not support export ciphersuites and so these
arguments are unused and can be ignored by the callback.
The callback should return the parameters to be used in a DH object.
Ownership of the DH object is retained by the application and should
later be freed.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
All of these functions/macros return 1 for success or 0 on error.
